What is Git?

Git is a Distributed Version Control System (DVCS) used to track changes in source code and help developers collaborate on projects.

In simple words:

Git is a tool that records every change you make to your code, allowing you to go back to previous versions, work with a team, and manage multiple features without affecting the main project.



Before Git, developers faced many problems:

If someone accidentally deleted code, it was difficult to recover.
Multiple developers editing the same files caused conflicts.
There was no history of who changed what.
Sharing code usually meant sending ZIP files or copying folders.
Working on new features without affecting the existing application was difficult.

Git solves all of these problems.

What does Git help us with?
1. Version Control

Git saves every version of your project.

Example:

Version 1 -> Login Page
Version 2 -> Registration Added
Version 3 -> Forgot Password Added
Version 4 -> Bug Fixed

If version 4 has a bug, you can return to version 3.

2. Track Changes

Git records

What changed
Who changed it
When it was changed
Why it was changed (through commit messages)

Example



Commit 2
Fixed NullPointerException

Commit 3
Added JWT Authentication

So months later you can easily understand the project's history.

3. Team Collaboration

Suppose three developers are working.

Developer A -> Login Module

Developer B -> Payment Module

Developer C -> Inventory Module

All can work at the same time without overwriting each other's code.

Git later combines (merges) everyone's work.

f your laptop crashes, you can clone the project again from the remote repository.

