8. Code Review

Before merging code into the main branch, teammates can review it.

This helps:

Find bugs
Improve code quality
Ensure coding standards are followed
Real-world Example

Imagine you're building an Inventory Management System.

Developer A:

Product CRUD

Developer B:

Authentication

Developer C:

Reports

Developer D:

Swagger Documentation

Without Git:

Everyone edits the same project.
Files get overwritten.
Changes are lost.

With Git:

main

|
|---- feature/product

|
|---- feature/auth

|
|---- feature/report

|
|---- feature/swagger

Each developer works independently. Once their work is tested and approved, it is merged into the main branch.

Benefits of Git
Tracks every code change.
Allows multiple developers to work together.
Prevents accidental loss of code.
Makes it easy to restore older versions.
Supports branching for feature development.
Maintains a complete project history.
Integrates with platforms like GitHub, GitLab, and Bitbucket.
Enables code reviews and continuous integration (CI/CD).